{% set local_problem %}
<p>Hartselle's varied property types create different drainage challenges. In-town lots often have grading that directs water toward homes rather than away. The larger properties outside town have their own patterns influenced by agricultural land use and Morgan County's soil composition.</p>

<p>Surface drainage solutions have limits. Splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. But on many <a href="/recent-projects/">Hartselle properties</a>, that's not far enough. Water that stays near your foundation — even a few feet away — can still find its way back during extended rainy periods on our clay-heavy soil.</p>

<p>We've seen the results throughout our hometown: wet basements, crawlspace moisture, foundation cracks, mold issues. Homeowners try various surface solutions without lasting success because the fundamental problem isn't being addressed — the water isn't going far enough away.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve this permanently. Instead of moving water a few feet and hoping for the best, buried pipes carry it far enough that it can never threaten your home.</p>

<p><strong>The investment perspective:</strong> Underground drains for a typical Hartselle home run $1,200-$2,400. Foundation repair runs $10,000-30,000. We've watched neighbors spend years dealing with moisture problems, trying cheaper fixes, before finally installing underground drains and solving the issue. Should've done it first.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes connecting to your downspouts and carrying water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. They work continuously and automatically — every time it rains, water routes away before it can threaten your foundation.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 4-inch Schedule 40 PVC pipe with smooth interior walls. Proper slope (1-2% grade) ensuring water flows continuously. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits at discharge points. Clean-out access for future maintenance.</p>

<p><strong>What we don't install:</strong> Corrugated flex pipe. This is critical. That black corrugated drainage pipe from Home Depot seems like a cheap alternative, but the corrugations trap sediment and debris. Roots grow into the ridges. Within 3-5 years, most corrugated pipe is partially or completely clogged.</p>

<p>We dig up failed corrugated systems around Hartselle constantly — homeowners thought they solved the problem, but created a new one. Smooth PVC costs more upfront but actually works for decades.</p>

<p><strong>The distinction:</strong> Underground gutter drains handle roof runoff. French drains handle groundwater already in soil. Different systems for different problems. Some Hartselle properties need both. We assess what's actually going on and recommend accordingly.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage throughout <a href="/recent-projects/">Hartselle and Morgan County</a> for over 20 years. We understand local soil conditions, typical drainage patterns, and what solutions actually work on different property types around here.</p>

<p>This is our hometown. When we install a drainage system for a Hartselle neighbor, we're going to see that property for years to come. That's motivation to do the job right the first time — and to be here if you ever need us again.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Every spring we dig up corrugated pipe that failed. Homeowners installed it themselves or hired a landscaper who didn't know better. The pipe is clogged, roots have invaded, water isn't flowing. They end up paying twice — once for the failed system, once for us to replace it with smooth PVC. Do it right the first time.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If you're spending money on underground drainage, spend the extra 20-30% for smooth PVC. The corrugated stuff isn't cheaper when you account for the fact that it fails and needs replacement. This is one place where "saving money" costs more in the long run.</p>
